include "../compiler/runtime/elisacore_std/elisacore_runtime.elisa"

# stage1 gap: printing an integer through the std declined the whole unit.
# stage0 compiled and ran this file; stage1 reported
#
#   error: backend emitted no functions for this unit; declined 40:
#     store_get, store_count, store_get, store_count, store_find, ...
#   note: every function in the unit is dropped when the backend declines one
#
# (Companion repro: nw_print_int_generic_stage1.elisa, same shape.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATUS: the generic-impl half is FIXED (2026-08-25, nw-port stage1 552bddca)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 40 declines were TWO independent bugs sharing one symptom. Measured against
# the pre-fix binary: 12 of the 40 were the generic protocol impls, the other 28
# were a `cstr` return-conversion gap that had nothing to do with them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUG 1 -- generic protocol impls (FIXED)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIRMED as hypothesised. Parser::impl_declaration
# (elisa-compiler-nw/src/parser/parser_decl_impl.elisa:17) matched only the
# three-bare-identifier header form:
#
#     if parser.peek() == Ident and parser.peek_at(1) == Ident and parser.peek_at(2) == Ident:
#
# i.e. `impl Holder for Box:`. For `impl[T] Store for darray[T]:` the tokens after
# `impl` are `[ T ] Store for darray [ T ]`, so peek() was LBracket, the branch
# never fired, and the `[T]` was skipped with the rest of the header by
# skip_declaration_header_clauses -- captured nowhere. Downstream,
# codegen_declare.elisa:193 declare_function_impl resolved the member's
# `darray[T]&` with no binding for T, got TypeKind.Unmodeled and returned null.
#
# THE FIX consumes the optional `[params]` after `impl` and records each parameter
# against every member `def`'s line on the side table function-level generics
# already use (generic_param_lines / generic_param_names). A member is then a
# generic TEMPLATE exactly as if written `def store_get[T](s: darray[T]&, ...)`:
# record_generic_func_metadata registers it, decl_line_is_generic keeps the eager
# declare/emit passes off it, resolve_declarations binds T in its body, and the
# existing UFCS template-selection path (codegen_call.elisa:764) instantiates it
# per concrete receiver. No parallel mechanism. stage0 likewise emits no body for
# an uninstantiated template -- `nm` on its object for this file shows no
# `store_*` symbol at all.
#
# Consuming the `[params]` also lets the existing three-identifier branch fire, so
# `__impl_scope` / `__impl_concrete` are recorded for generic impls now too.
#
# END TO END, not just "no longer declines": with
#
#     def total[S: Store](s: S&) -> usize:
#         return s.store_count()
#
# a `darray[i64]` dispatches through `impl[T] Store for darray[T]` and answers its
# count on stage1, agreeing with stage0. Covered by
# elisa-compiler-nw/test/parity/generic_protocol_impl_smoke.sh.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUG 2 -- `u8&` returned in a `cstr` context (separate; see the companion repro)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The other 28 declines were the std's `impl Str for X: def __cast__(...) -> cstr`
# members plus the `cstr[shape_*]` forwarders in runtime.elisa. Nothing to do with
# generic impls: a call returning `heap u8&` in a `cstr` context was auto-
# DEREFERENCED to an `i8` and the return path then refused it. Root cause and fix
# in repro/nw_cstr_ref_return_stage1.elisa.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFIED (unchanged from the original investigation)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * `print("text")` alone: fine on both stages -- and it always was, with 24
#   declines silently recorded in the module's `!elisa.declined` metadata. A
#   decline is only fatal once something REFERENCES it
#   (codegen_module.elisa:257-260), which is why the two bugs stayed invisible
#   until `print(42)` reached them.
# * Ordinary darray work (push, index, iterate) is unaffected -- those are builtin
#   container ops, not protocol dispatch.
# * stage1's own source contains ZERO generic impls (`grep -rn '^impl\[' src/` is
#   empty), which is why its self-host fixpoint never exercised this path. The
#   only generic impls in the ecosystem are the three in the std:
#     impl[T] Store for darray[T]        (collections.elisa:1474)
#     impl[T] Store for Deque[T]         (collections.elisa:1485)
#     impl[K, T] KeyedStore for dict[K, T] (collections.elisa:1505)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STILL OPEN -- and this file still declines, for ONE remaining reason
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * `print(42)` reaches the std's generic `print[T: Str]`, whose body is
#   `print(T.__cast__(value))` -- static protocol dispatch through a bound type
#   parameter. stage1 resolves that by matching the argument types against the
#   impl members, and requires the match to be UNIQUE. It is not: `char`, `int`,
#   `i64` and `isize` are ONE ValueType to this backend (Signed/64, see
#   codegen_env.elisa scalar_type_of_name), so `impl Str for char`,
#   `impl Str for int` and `impl Str for i64` are indistinguishable and the call
#   declines rather than guessing. An interim version DID guess and made
#   `print(42)` print "*" -- chr(42), through the char impl -- which is why the
#   rule is unique-or-decline.
#
#   Closing it needs type ARGUMENTS to carry name-level identity (or `char` to
#   become a distinct ValueType), not more resolution cleverness. Until then
#   `print(<integer>)`, `print(<float>)` and `print(<char>)` decline while
#   `print(<string>)` works. Decline count for this file: 40 before this work,
#   5 after (`join`, `print__i64`, `print`, `print__u32`, `print`).
#
# * DIVERGENT DIAGNOSTICS on rejected programs. For an INVALID direct call such as
#   `store_count(&xs)` (a protocol method is reached through a bound generic, not
#   by free call -- docs/69), stage0 emits `undefined identifier "store_count"`
#   while stage1 silently declines the unit. Same for `__cast__(42)`, which stage0
#   rejects and stage1 accepts. stage1's decline report is also capped at 12 names
#   (codegen_debug.elisa:458) with no way to ask for the rest -- reading the full
#   list means `-emit llvm` on a unit that still emits, then grepping
#   `!elisa.declined`.

def main() -> i64:
    can Console.Write, Memory.Allocate, Console.Format, Abort.Panic:
        print(42)
        return 0
